-- Q3320: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both amounts are reported as revenue and expense on government-wide statements","The new bonds create a liability while the old bonds remain until legally defeased","No adjustment is needed because fund and government-wide treatments are identical","The old bonds are immediately removed from the government-wide balance sheet"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 65, when a government advance refunds debt, the government-wide statements record the new bonds as a long-term liability while the old bonds remain until legally defeased. The difference between the reacquisition price and the net carrying amount is recognized as a deferred outflow or inflow of resources.
Wrong (A): Bond proceeds and escrow payments are not reported as revenue and expense; they are financing activities on the fund statements and liability adjustments on government-wide statements.
Wrong (C): An adjustment is required because governmental funds report proceeds and payments as other financing sources/uses, while government-wide statements use full accrual.
Wrong (D): The old bonds are not immediately removed; they remain as liabilities until the escrow legally defeases them.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3320;

-- Q3323: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Add the increase in supplies inventory to reconcile to government-wide basis","Subtract the decrease in supplies inventory to reconcile to government-wide basis","Add the net increase because government-wide records supplies as assets until consumed","No adjustment is needed because supplies are treated the same in both statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 78, when governmental funds use the purchases method, supplies are expensed when purchased. Government-wide statements use the consumption method, expensing supplies when used. A net increase in inventory means fewer supplies were consumed than purchased, so the excess must be added back to reconcile.
Wrong (A): Simply adding the increase is incomplete without recognizing the measurement focus difference between purchases and consumption methods.
Wrong (B): Subtracting a decrease would be the wrong direction; a decrease means more was consumed than purchased.
Wrong (D): Supplies are not treated the same — funds use purchases basis while government-wide uses consumption basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3323;

-- Q3324: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Amortized over the bond term using the effective interest method","Reported as other financing sources in the period of issuance","Not recorded in the governmental fund financial statements","Reported as deferred revenue to be recognized over the bond term"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraphs 77-79, bond premiums received at issuance are reported as other financing sources in governmental funds alongside the face amount of the bonds. They are not amortized because governmental funds use the current financial resources measurement focus.
Wrong (A): Amortization over the bond term occurs on the government-wide statements under the economic resources measurement focus, not in governmental funds.
Wrong (C): The premium is recorded in governmental funds — as an other financing source in the issuance period.
Wrong (D): A bond premium is not deferred revenue; it is a financing source recognized immediately in the fund statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3324;

-- Q3327: Interfund Transactions [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A reciprocal exchange of goods or services between two separate funds","A one-way flow of resources from one fund to another without repayment","A temporary loan between funds that must be repaid within one year","A payment for services rendered by one fund to another at market rates"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, an interfund transfer is a one-way movement of resources between funds without a corresponding return flow of assets, services, or repayment. Transfers are reported as other financing sources in the receiving fund and other financing uses in the providing fund.
Wrong (A): A reciprocal exchange of goods or services describes an interfund service provided and used, not a transfer.
Wrong (C): A temporary loan between funds is an interfund advance or borrowing, which creates receivable and payable balances.
Wrong (D): A payment for services at market rates is an interfund service provided and used, treated like an external transaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3327;

-- Q3329: Interfund Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both are classified as transfers because all interfund flows are nonreciprocal resource movements","The IT payment is a transfer reported below the operating line; the road funding is an operating expenditure","Both are reported as revenues and expenses because GASB treats all interfund activity as quasi-external","The IT payment is revenue/expense (exchange at market rates); the road transfer is other financing sources/uses (nonreciprocal)"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): GASB Statement No. 34 distinguishes interfund services provided and used from interfund transfers based on whether a genuine exchange occurs. The IT services payment is exchange-like at market rates, so it is reported as revenue in the General Fund and expense in the Enterprise Fund. The road transfer is nonreciprocal, reported as other financing sources/uses.
Wrong (A): Not all interfund flows are transfers; GASB distinguishes exchange-type services from nonreciprocal transfers.
Wrong (B): This reverses the classification — the IT payment is the exchange transaction reported as revenue/expense, not a transfer.
Wrong (C): GASB does not treat all interfund activity as quasi-external; only genuine exchange transactions receive revenue/expense treatment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3329;

-- Q3330: Interfund Transactions [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Revenue recognized in the fund providing the reimbursement payment","Other financing sources reported below the operating expenditure line","Transfers between the two funds reported as financing activities","A reduction of expenditures or expenses in the fund that is reimbursed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB Statement No. 34, an interfund reimbursement occurs when one fund pays for a cost that properly belongs to another fund. The reimbursement reduces expenditures in the fund initially charged (general fund) and records the expenditure in the fund that should bear the cost (special revenue fund).
Wrong (A): Reimbursements are not revenue — they are cost corrections that restore expenditures to the proper fund.
Wrong (B): Other financing sources applies to transfers, not reimbursements that correct misclassified expenditures.
Wrong (C): Reimbursements are distinct from transfers; they correct errors rather than move resources as a policy decision.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3330;

-- Q3332: Interfund Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An expenditure reported in the operating section of the statement","An other financing use reported as a transfer out below the operating line","A liability owed to the debt service fund until settlement occurs","A reduction of revenue offset against the General Fund''s tax collections"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, transfers out are reported as other financing uses on the fund statement of revenues, expenditures, and changes in fund balances. They appear below the operating section and reduce fund balance. The receiving debt service fund reports a corresponding other financing source.
Wrong (A): Transfers are not expenditures; they are classified separately as other financing uses below the excess/deficiency line.
Wrong (C): A transfer is not a liability — it is a completed resource movement, not an obligation to pay.
Wrong (D): Transfers do not reduce revenue; they are a distinct category reported after the excess/deficiency of revenues over expenditures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3332;

-- Q3333: Interfund Transactions [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A transfer out in the General Fund and a transfer in for Capital Projects","Revenue in the Capital Projects Fund and an expenditure in the General Fund","A receivable in the General Fund and a payable in the Capital Projects Fund","An expenditure in the General Fund reported in the operating activity section"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 112a, an interfund loan creates reciprocal receivable and payable balances: a ''due from other funds'' in the lending General Fund and a ''due to other funds'' in the borrowing Capital Projects Fund. These are balance sheet items that do not affect revenues or expenditures.
Wrong (A): A transfer implies a permanent one-way resource movement; loans are temporary and must be repaid.
Wrong (B): Loans do not generate revenue or expenditures — they create reciprocal balance sheet accounts.
Wrong (D): An interfund loan is not an expenditure; it is a temporary advance creating an asset in the lending fund.$EXPL$
