-- Q3361: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Liability awards remeasured at fair value each reporting period","Equity awards measured only at the grant date fair value","Revenue recognized over the three-year vesting service period","Off-balance-sheet items disclosed only in the financial statement notes"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 718, cash-settled SARs are liability-classified awards. The liability is remeasured at fair value each reporting period until settlement, with changes recognized as compensation expense. This differs from equity-classified awards measured only at the grant date.
Wrong (B): Equity classification applies to share-settled awards, not cash-settled SARs that create an obligation to pay cash.
Wrong (C): Stock compensation is an expense, not revenue; SARs create compensation cost recognized over the vesting period.
Wrong (D): Cash-settled SARs must be recognized as liabilities on the balance sheet, not disclosed off-balance-sheet.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3361;

-- Q3363: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Full fair value of the options recognized over the service period","Zero because the performance condition is not yet probable of achievement","Half the fair value as a conservative estimate of probable achievement","The intrinsic value of the options at the end of the reporting period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 718, when a performance condition is not probable of achievement, no compensation expense is recognized. Once the condition becomes probable, cumulative catch-up expense is recognized for the elapsed portion of the service period. Performance conditions affect the number of awards expected to vest.
Wrong (A): Full fair value recognition requires that the performance condition be probable; expense is deferred until probability is established.
Wrong (C): ASC 718 does not allow a conservative partial estimate; it is an all-or-nothing assessment based on probability of achievement.
Wrong (D): Stock options with performance conditions are measured at grant-date fair value, not intrinsic value at period end.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3363;

-- Q3365: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the Black-Scholes model without any adjustment to term inputs","The vesting period only as the expected term for option valuation","The contractual term only as the expected term for option valuation","The simplified method using the midpoint between vesting date and contractual term"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under SEC Staff Accounting Bulletin guidance and ASC 718, the simplified method estimates expected term as (vesting period + contractual term) / 2. This is available when a company lacks sufficient historical exercise data. For example, options vesting in 4 years with a 10-year term yield an expected term of 7 years.
Wrong (A): Black-Scholes is a pricing model, not a method for estimating expected term; expected term is an input to the model.
Wrong (B): Using only the vesting period would underestimate the expected term, as employees typically hold options beyond the vesting date.
Wrong (C): Using only the contractual term would overestimate expected term, as employees rarely hold options until expiration.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3365;

-- Q3367: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The contingency is assessed as remote under the ASC 450 probability threshold","The fair value of the contingent liability can be determined at acquisition date","Only after the contingency is fully resolved through settlement or judgment","Contingent liabilities are never recognized separately in a business combination"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 805, contingent liabilities assumed in a business combination are recognized at fair value at the acquisition date if fair value can be determined during the measurement period. This is a lower threshold than ASC 450, which generally requires probability of loss for recognition.
