-- Q3375: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Can designate the hedge retroactively to the inception date","Cannot apply hedge accounting and must report all fair value changes in earnings","Must wait until the next fiscal year to formally designate the hedge","Can apply hedge accounting after a mandatory 30-day waiting period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 815, hedge accounting requires formal designation and documentation at inception of the hedge relationship. If the entity fails to designate at inception, hedge accounting cannot be applied retroactively. All changes in the derivative''s fair value are recognized in current earnings.
Wrong (A): Retroactive designation is not permitted; ASC 815 requires contemporaneous documentation at inception.
Wrong (C): There is no next-fiscal-year waiting provision; the entity may designate a new hedge relationship prospectively at any time with proper documentation.
Wrong (D): ASC 815 contains no 30-day waiting period; designation must occur at inception or not at all for that specific hedge relationship.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3375;

-- Q3377: Derivatives and Hedging [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reclassified to earnings immediately upon discontinuation of hedge accounting","Written off against retained earnings as a prior period adjustment","Reversed against the derivative''s fair value on the balance sheet","Remain in AOCI and reclassified to earnings when the forecasted transaction occurs"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 815, when a cash flow hedge is discontinued because it is no longer effective but the forecasted transaction is still expected to occur, amounts previously recorded in AOCI remain there. They are reclassified to earnings in the same period(s) the forecasted transaction affects earnings.
Wrong (A): Immediate reclassification would only occur if the forecasted transaction is no longer expected to occur, which is not the case here.
Wrong (B): AOCI amounts from discontinued hedges are not written off against retained earnings; they follow the forecasted transaction timing.
Wrong (C): The AOCI balance is not reversed against the derivative; it remains in equity until the forecasted transaction affects earnings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3377;

-- Q3380: State and Local Government Reporting [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Long-term liabilities reported only on the government-wide financial statements","Short-term liabilities reported in the governmental fund receiving the proceeds","Off-balance-sheet financing disclosed only in the notes to the statements","Deferred revenue recognized over the period until tax collections arrive"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Tax anticipation notes are short-term borrowings expected to be repaid from tax collections within the current fiscal period. Because they mature within the current period, they meet the current financial resources measurement focus and are reported as fund liabilities in governmental funds.
Wrong (A): Because these notes mature within the current period, they are not classified as long-term debt; they appear in the fund statements as current liabilities.
Wrong (C): Tax anticipation notes are recognized on the balance sheet as liabilities, not treated as off-balance-sheet financing.
Wrong (D): Borrowing proceeds are not deferred revenue; they create a liability that will be repaid from future tax collections.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3380;

